%[text] %[text:anchor:T_1010] # Functions
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text] Organizing code into functions improves the readability, reusability, and ease of testing your code. A function takes some inputs; runs code in a local scope that might produce side effects like creating a plot; and returns some outputs that will then be available to the rest of your program.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A function diagram with a white box labeled "inputs" with an arrow into ](text:image:77c4)
%[text] This script addresses built-in functions, refactoring code to create functions, local functions, functions defined in their own files, programmatic scope, the MATLAB path, and how to pass functions as arguments to other functions.
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3260] ## Programming Languages as Calculators
%[text] Programming languages can behave as basic calculators but are far more flexible than your basic four-function calculator. Nearly all programming languages have built-in functions in addition to simple arithmetic, and any specialized mathematical programming language will have far more, including support for complex numbers and built-in constants such as $\\pi${"altText":"pi"}. The challenge is tracking and informing the computer about the data type involved in each computation. 
%%
