%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6799] ### Composition of Functions
%[text] As with mathematical functions, programmatic functions can be composed whenever the output type of the inner function is the input type of the argument for the outer function. 
(cosd(30))^2
x = 1:100;
plot(x,sqrt(x))
%[text] As in mathematics, there are limitations on the ability to compose functions in a programming language. Try running the following code after pasting it into the code box below. What do you expect to happen?
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] str1 = "cat";
%[text] str2 = "dog";
%[text] sqrt(str1+str2)
%[text] ```


%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_2ef7] ### Assignment Operator
%[text] Every programming language has some way of assigning expressions to names that can be used later in the code in place of that expression. Just as in algebra, you can use a single symbol to represent a function, $y = 3x^2-7x+1${"altText":"y = 3x^2-7x+1"}, that can then be used inside other functions:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f = \\frac{e^{y/3}}{-0.6y^2+\\cos(y)} = \\frac{e^{x^2-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{1}{3}}}{-5.4x^4+25.2x^3-33x^2+8.4x-.6+\\cos(3x^2-7x+1)}${"altText":"f = exp(y\/3)\/(-0.6y^2 + cos(y)) = exp(x^2 - (7\/2)x + 1\/3)\/(-5.4x^4 + 25.2x^3 - 33x^2 + 8.4x - 0.6 + cos(3x^2 - 7x + 1))"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A function diagram showing an orange box labeled "x" with an arrow to a blue box labeled "y" with an arrow to an orange box labeled "y(x)" with an arrow to a blue box labeled "f" with an arrow to an orange box labeled "f(y(x))".](text:image:50ca)
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_55D617CE] Such assignment allows the programmer to build up complex operations step-by-step. In MATLAB, a variable is assigned to a value using the symbol `=`. 
%[text] ![](text:image:47e6) **Exercise 3.**
%[text] Calculate the value of $f${"altText":"f"} if $x=0${"altText":"x=0"}, $x=4.3${"altText":"x=4.3"}, $x=2.18045${"altText":"x=2.18045"} and $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi}}{2}${"altText":"x = -sqrt(pi)\/2"}. 

%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5cba] ## Using Refactor
%[text] A traditional function to code in any new programming language is to print the phrase "Hello, world!". Simply printing this phrase is easy:
disp("Hello, world!")
%%
%[text] How could we create a script that would greet a user by name? First, define a variable called `name`.
name = "Sara" %[text:anchor:M_91D969C4]
%[text] Then use this name to display the string you want:
disp("Hello, " + name + "!") %[text:anchor:M_C9A1D998]
%[text] Select the ["Hello, name!"](internal:M_C9A1D998) code, and on the **Live Editor** tab, in the **Code** section, click ![Code refactor icon](text:image:15a8) **Refactor** and select **Convert from Local Function** from the available options. This will cut and paste this code to the end of the script as a function called `untitled`. Fix the name to be more descriptive, such as `helloWorld`, and then scroll back here. What happened to your code? Does it still work the way you expect if you run it?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8019] ### Inputs, Outputs & Side Effects
%[text] The `helloWorld` function has one input, `name`, one side effect, printing a string to the display, and no outputs.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![A function diagram showing a white box labeled "name" with an arrow to a blue box labeled "](text:image:346f)
%[text]  For each function you write, you must carefully document what values it requires to run. Pass the required values in as a list of arguments. You also need to consider what values you want to have after running the script and define those to be the output values for your function. Finally, you want to decide what side effects or impacts running the function will have on other parts of the system. 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_445f] ## Local Functions
%[text] If there is a set of operations that you know will repeat, or that form a logical unit of code, you can define a local function, as with h`elloWorld`. For example, to define a function named `functionName` with three input values, `arg1`, `arg2`, and `arg3`, and two output values, `out1` and `out2`, the MATLAB syntax is: 
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] function [out1,out2] = functionName(arg1,arg2,arg3)
%[text]     % Simple, one-line description of functionName
%[text]     % More detailed description of how to use functionName
%[text]    Code 
%[text] end
%[text] ```
%[text] Other programming languages use different syntax, but they all have some way of identifying input values, output values, a name for the function, and what the program should do when the function is called. 
%[text] Within a live script, local functions must be defined at the end of the file and called anywhere throughout the live script.
%[text] ![](text:image:305b) **Try**. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_BA6CEAAE] 1. Define a function called `quadFormula` that implements the quadratic root-finding formula. Given a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c = 0${"altText":"ax^2+bx+c = 0"}, the solutions are: \
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_3C79F8D0] $x\_+ = \\frac{-b + \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}${"altText":"x\_+ = (-b + sqrt(b^2 - 4ac))\/(2a)"} and $x\_- = \\frac{-b-\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}${"altText":"x\_- = (-b - sqrt(b^2 - 4ac))\/(2a)"}
%[text] That means your function takes three arguments, `a`, `b`, and `c`, and returns two values, `xPlus` and `xMinus.` 
% Write your quadFormula function here
 




%[text]           2\. Can you simplify the code for `quadFormula` by defining another function called `discriminant` that only calculates the value of $\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}${"altText":"sqrt(b^2 - 4ac)"}? Notice that functions can call other functions, just as you can call functions from the command line or in a script. This is the programmatic version of function composition. 



%[text] Once you have defined a function, you can call that function using the name you gave it just as you would any other function within the language. 
% Uncomment and run this code after you have defined quadFormula
% With a = 1, b = 2, c = -3, the correct values are 
% xPlus = 1 and xMinus = -3 
a = 1;
b = 2; 
c = -3; 

% [xPlus,xMinus] = quadFormula(a,b,c)
%[text] ![](text:image:8368) **Reflect**. 
%[text] 1. How can you check if you have implemented your function correctly? 
%[text] 2. What happens if you pass in incorrect values, such as the strings `"a"`, `"b"`, and `"c"` rather than numerical values for the coefficients? What happens if you pass numerical values directly, e.g., `quadFormula(1,2,-3)`?
%[text] 3. What happens if you call the function with different names for the output values? Perhaps you use `r1` and `r2,` or `x1` and `x2,` instead of `xPlus` and `xMinus`?
%[text] 4. What happens if you call `quadFormula` with only one output variable defined? Or no output variables at all?
%[text] 5. What happens if you call `quadFormula` with three output variables defined?
%[text] 6. Does `quadFormula` work in both this script and the Command Window? \
%[text] Additional test equations: 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $x^2-9=0$,      $6x^2+11x-10=0$,      and      $x^2+x+1=0$
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3092] ## Function Files
%[text] In other cases, we may want to define [functions](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_prog/create-functions-in-files.html) that will be accessible from several different scripts or separate the functions into different files and folders to create nicely organized code. You can create functions in MATLAB as either live script `(*.mlx)` or M-files `(*.m).` 
%[text] ![](text:image:8642) **Try**. 
%[text] 1. Create a new live script on the **Live Editor** tab of the toolstrip by selecting ![](text:image:1499)**New** and then ![](text:image:1323)**Live Script**. Alternately, you can select the tab marked + at the top of the Live Editor window. 
%[text] 2. Cut and paste the function (and subfunctions, if they exist) into the new `.mlx` file. Save the new file with the same name as the function, in this case, `quad.m`.
%[text] 3. Use your new function here, and test that it now works in the Command Window. \
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] [r1,r2] = quad(2,0,-1)
%[text] ```

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:3fc5) **Reflect**. 
%[text] 1. When does defining a function as part of a script make sense? 
%[text] 2. When might you want to use inline commands? Or functions defined in separate files?  \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9b9f] ## Scope
%[text] Where can you use `quad`? Does it work inside this live script? On the command line in the Command Window? From a different live script?  The answers to these questions are related to the concept of the *scope* of a function or variable. Computers must be told where to find the commands or data you want them to use.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_6413] ### MATLAB Path
%[text] In MATLAB, the scope of a function is related to the idea of the [MATLAB path](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/matlab_env/what-is-the-matlab-search-path.html), which is the set of files, in order, that should be searched to determine if there is a function with a given name defined. You can programmatically change the [MATLAB search path](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/search-path.html) using commands in a script, function, or the Command Window. You can also use the context menus of the Graphical User Interface by right-clicking on folders in the Current Folder panel. If you have implemented a function, but it is not on your search path, then MATLAB will not find it, and you will likely get an error when you try to use it. 
%[text] ![Warning Symbol](text:image:8488) This does mean that defining a local function of your own can overload a function name so that your version of a function will be called rather than the standard MATLAB version. This is why you may want to check the MATLAB documentation to see if there is a function you want to use that has the same name and arguments as a function you want to define. If you want to know which implementation of a function is found first, you can use the command `which`.
which quad
which -all quad
%[text] ![](text:image:5d67) **Try**. Can you check the documentation to determine what the MATLAB `quad` function does? What command does the documentation suggest that you use instead?
%[text] ![](text:image:3709) **Reflect**. 
%[text] 1. What do you think it means for functions to be **`Shadowed`** in MATLAB? 
%[text] 2. How does MATLAB choose which version of `quad` to use when there is more than one on the MATLAB Path?
%[text] 3. Is it possible that you could call a different `quad` than you meant to call without realizing it? Why or Why not?
%[text] 4. Why might you want to restrict the scope of a variable or a function? \
%[text] ![](text:image:9748) **Try**.
%[text] Move the `quad.m` file into the `FunctionLibrary` folder. Try running `quad.m` [here](internal:M_5b4e) and in the Command Window. Then right-click `FunctionLibrary` and select **Remove from path \> Selected folders**. Try running `quad.m` again. Right-click `FunctionLibrary` and select **Add to path \> Selected folders** before you continue with this script. 
